    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">A Reverse Mortgage Is Not a Last Resort: How Homeowners Are Using It as a Retirement Planning Tool</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you're financially stable, have retirement savings, and assumed a reverse mortgage was designed for people in financial difficulty rather than for someone in your position, this article will reframe how you think about it. Financial planners have been incorporating reverse mortgages into retirement strategies for well-resourced homeowners for years. Most of their clients had no idea that was even possible.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Image Problem This Product Has Never Fully Solved</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Reverse mortgages carry a reputation as a product of last resort, something you turn to when the retirement account is empty and the bills are mounting. That reputation is outdated and, for many borrowers, simply inaccurate. The product has been significantly reformed, the FHA-insured HECM version carries meaningful consumer protections, and a growing body of research in retirement planning literature treats it as a legitimate tool for managing sequence-of-returns risk, preserving investment portfolios, and creating flexible income in retirement. The homeowners who benefit most from that research are often the ones who never look into it because they assume it isn't for them.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Why Home Equity Is the Largest Untapped Asset in Most Retirement Plans</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here's what most retirement conversations miss. For many homeowners 62 and older, home equity represents the largest single asset on their personal balance sheet, often larger than their retirement accounts. Yet most retirement income planning treats the home as a fixed, untouchable asset and builds income strategies entirely around investment portfolios and Social Security. A reverse mortgage line of credit changes that by making home equity a usable, flexible resource without requiring a sale.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The strategic value comes from optionality. A reverse mortgage line of credit that you open but don't immediately draw on grows over time at a rate tied to interest. It becomes a larger resource the longer it sits unused. That growth is not dependent on home value appreciation. It happens regardless of what the housing market does, and it creates a financial buffer that can be deployed selectively when other assets are underperforming.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How Retirement Planners Actually Use This Tool</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Several strategies have emerged in retirement planning literature that treat a reverse mortgage as a coordinated part of a broader income plan rather than a standalone product.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Portfolio protection during market downturns.</strong> Instead of selling investments at a loss during a down market to cover living expenses, a homeowner draws from the reverse mortgage line of credit. The portfolio recovers. The line of credit is repaid later if desired, or the balance simply accrues. Withdrawing from a depressed portfolio to fund living expenses is one of the primary ways retirees exhaust savings prematurely. A reverse mortgage line of credit can interrupt that pattern.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Delaying Social Security.</strong> Every year you delay claiming Social Security past full retirement age increases your monthly benefit by approximately 8%. For homeowners who could use income to bridge that delay but don't want to draw down investments, a reverse mortgage provides a source of funds that doesn't require selling assets.</p>

    <p class="mt-4"><strong>Supplementing a fixed income.</strong> For homeowners whose retirement income covers essentials but leaves little margin for unexpected expenses, medical costs, or quality-of-life spending, a reverse mortgage line of credit provides accessible capital without a monthly repayment obligation.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Evaluate your home equity as a retirement asset, not just as a place you live.</strong> If it represents a significant portion of your net worth and you have no current plan to access it, that's worth examining with the same rigor you'd apply to any other asset.</li>
      <li><strong>Talk to a fee-only financial planner who has experience with reverse mortgages in retirement planning.</strong> Not all financial planners are familiar with how these products integrate into a broader plan. Find one who is before making any decisions.</li>
      <li><strong>Consider opening a reverse mortgage line of credit earlier rather than later.</strong> The line grows over time regardless of whether you draw on it. Opening it at 62 or 65 and letting it grow gives you a larger resource to draw on at 75 or 80 if you need it.</li>
      <li><strong>Understand how a reverse mortgage interacts with your other income sources.</strong> Draws from a reverse mortgage are generally not considered taxable income. That has implications for tax planning, Medicare premium calculations, and Social Security taxation that are worth discussing with a financial or tax professional.</li>
      <li><strong>Complete the required HUD counseling with a strategic mindset.</strong> Use it as an opportunity to ask questions about how the product fits your specific financial picture, not just whether you qualify.</li>
    </ol>

